  "Question": "A rational number between \\(\\sqrt2\\) and \\(\\sqrt3\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{\\sqrt2+\\sqrt3}{2}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{\\sqrt2\\cdot\\sqrt3}{2}\\)",
    "C": "\\(1.5\\)",
    "D": "\\(1.8\\)",
    "E": "\\(1.4\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "The number \\(1.5=\\frac32\\) is rational because it is a ratio of integers. To locate it between the two positive square roots, compare its square with their radicands:\n\\[\n2<\\left(\\frac32\\right)^2=\\frac94<3.\n\\]\nSquaring preserves the order of positive numbers, so these inequalities give \\(\\sqrt2<1.5<\\sqrt3\\). The answer is \\(1.5\\), choice C.",
